WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:00.000
You may have heard that money can't always buy happiness. Well, that's especially true for children from affluent families who may feel pressure to succeed, get good grades, and be modeled children. In this episode, we talk with one psychologist who is teaching her own children how to be happy without relying on money. I'm Audrey Hamilton, and this is speaking of psychology. Sunil Luther is a psychology professor at Arizona State University. She has studied the vulnerability and resilience of youth in poverty and children. and families affected by mental illness. Her most recent work has focused on children in affluent communities.
你可能听说过金钱并不总能买到幸福。对于来自富裕家庭的孩子来说，这尤其正确，因为他们可能感受到必须成功、取得好成绩并成为模范儿童的压力。在本期节目中，我们采访了一位心理学家，她正在教导自己的孩子如何在不依赖金钱的情况下获得幸福。我是奥黛丽·汉密尔顿，这是《心理学谈话》。苏尼尔·卢瑟是亚利桑那州立大学的心理学教授。她研究过贫困青少年以及受精神疾病影响的儿童和家庭的脆弱性与韧性。她最近的研究重点是富裕社区的儿童。

2
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Welcome, Dr. Luther. Ah, thank you for having me. Is depression very common among children from affluent families? You know, how do these depression rates compare to children from lower-income families? What we found already is rates of about one and a half to two and a half times as high as national normative samples. Of problems like depressive symptoms, anxiety symptoms, and even higher rates of problems. higher rates of problems of substance use, that would be alcohol, marijuana, and even heart drugs.
欢迎您，卢瑟博士。啊，谢谢你邀请我。抑郁症在富裕家庭的孩子中很常见吗？你知道，这些抑郁症发病率与低收入家庭的孩子相比如何？我们发现，这些症状的发病率大约是全国常模样本的1.5到2.5倍。像抑郁症状、焦虑症状等问题，甚至在药物滥用方面——也就是酒精、大麻，甚至是硬性毒品——问题发生率更高。

3
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Wow. So when you were studying this, was it a surprising finding to you? Oh, it was awfully surprising. Actually, I stumbled upon these findings quite by accident. My first study on upper-middle-class youth was conducted essentially to look for a comparison group for inner-city kids. And while doing this comparison, we found, much to my surprise, that the sort of the so-called privileged or rich kids were doing much more poorly, again, mostly on substance use,
哇。那么当你在研究这个问题时，这是一个让你感到惊讶的发现吗？哦，这非常令人惊讶。事实上，我完全是偶然发现了这些结果。我对中上层阶级青少年的第一次研究，本质上是为了寻找一个与内城孩子进行比较的群体。而在进行这种比较时，令我非常惊讶的是，我们发现所谓的特权阶层或富家子弟的情况要糟糕得多，同样，主要是在药物滥用方面，

4
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:35.000
but also in depression and anxiety. So that was way back in the late 90s, and since then we followed that trail and replicated these findings over and over again. What have you found to be the reason behind these high numbers? Can you describe the research a little bit more in detail on this area? Yes. People have a tendency to say, oh, it's the parents. And I cannot think of something that is more misguided than to pick on either the parents or the schools. There is not a single unitary factor that will explain all this. It is a problem that derives from multiple levels. Let's start with just American society and what our values are.
但也包括抑郁和焦虑。那是在90年代末，从那时起，我们沿着这条线索进行了多次重复验证。你认为这些高数字背后的原因是什么？你能更详细地描述一下这个领域的研究吗？是的。人们倾向于说，哦，都是父母的错。但我认为没有什么比指责父母或学校更误导人的了。没有单一的因素可以解释这一切。这是一个源于多个层面的问题。让我们从美国社会以及我们的价值观开始谈起。

5
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:48.000
More is better. Go ahead. The title of my last chapter, I can, therefore I must. If you can, therefore you must. Achieve more and move ahead. So this starts at the level of society. Then it goes into the schools. It goes into the universities.
越多越好。请继续。我最后一章的标题是：我能，所以我必须。如果你能，所以你必须。取得更多成就并不断前进。所以这始于社会层面。然后进入学校。再进入大学。

6
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:29.000
What is it that admissions people value in making their selections? It's all about accomplishments and achievements. So the bottom line is it is not just the family, it is not just the child, it is the culture we live in, the universities, the schools, everybody coming together to reinforce that one big message. If you can, therefore you must. Do not stop. So how does this relate to the problems of depression, substance, pressure to achieve? You see, if your sense of self-worth gets tied into how much you can accomplish, two things happen. One is if you don't accomplish, you feel small, inadequate, lousy, what have you. And the other is you live in a state of fear of not achieving.
招生人员在做选择时看重什么？全都是关于成就和功绩。所以底线是，这不仅仅是家庭的问题，也不仅仅是孩子的问题，而是我们所处的文化、大学、学校，所有人共同强化了那一个大信息。如果你能，所以你必须。不要停下来。那么这与抑郁、药物滥用、成就压力的问题有什么关系呢？你看，如果你的自我价值感与你能取得多少成就挂钩，就会发生两件事。一是如果你没有取得成就，你会感到渺小、无能、糟糕等等。另一件事是，你生活在一种对无法取得成就的恐惧状态中。

7
00:03:29.000 --> 00:04:16.000
If I can tell myself I'm a good person because I'm a good mother, a good friend, that's one thing. If I tell myself I'm a good person to the degree that I get that mixed grant or I get famous or I get these accolades, I'm living in fear. These are things that are not as much in my control as are things like being a good mother or being a good friend. So living in this constant state of tension, fear, if I don't achieve, who will I be? Is something that puts us in a state of anxiety and the failures in a state of depression. To self-medicate these feelings of anxiety and depression, unfortunately, a lot of our kids are turning to drugs and alcohol, as are we their parents. Is your advice that affluent parents simply lower the pressures they put on their children?
如果我能告诉自己我是一个好人，因为我是一个好母亲、好朋友，那是一回事。如果我告诉自己，我是一个好人，程度取决于我是否获得了那笔混合拨款，或者我是否出名，或者我是否获得了这些荣誉，那我就是在恐惧中生活。这些事情不像做一个好母亲或好朋友那样在我的掌控之中。因此，生活在这种持续的紧张和恐惧状态中——如果我不成功，我会是谁？——这让我们处于焦虑状态，而失败则让我们处于抑郁状态。为了自我治疗这些焦虑和抑郁的感觉，不幸的是，我们的许多孩子开始转向毒品和酒精，就像我们这些父母一样。你的建议是富裕的父母应该简单地降低他们给孩子施加的压力吗？

8
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Or how do they go about to balance the need? for success versus, you know, mental health. In terms of advice for parents, I'm thinking back on when we had first started doing in-depth research on families and poverty, right? Now, mothers in poverty living in inner city areas where there's a lot of violence and so on, they have to work extra hard to make sure that children do not hang out after dark in places that are dangerous. It almost becomes extra important for them to pay attention to this front.
或者他们该如何平衡对成功的需求与心理健康？关于给父母的建议，我回想起我们刚开始对贫困家庭进行深入研究的时候。在内城地区，暴力事件频发，贫困母亲们必须格外努力，确保孩子们在天黑后不会在危险的地方闲逛。对她们来说，关注这一方面变得格外重要。

9
00:04:47.000 --> 00:05:29.000
In much the same way, we have to be. parents in upper middle class societies have to work extra hard at ensuring that our children have a balanced value system, that they are not all about who can I be, how far can I go at whatever cost, but they are equally, if not more, invested in, how can I be a decent kind person, invested in the good of humanity and not just myself. So to summarize that, just as the inner city parents must work. extra hard to keep their children physically safe and out of trouble with gangs, we in upper middle class white-collar families need to work extra hard to make sure our children do not get
同样地，我们必须……中上层社会的父母必须格外努力，确保我们的孩子拥有平衡的价值观，他们不仅仅关注“我能成为谁”、“我不惜一切代价能走多远”，而是同样甚至更投入于“我如何成为一个体面、善良的人”，投入于人类的福祉，而不仅仅是自己。总结一下，就像内城区的父母必须格外努力以确保孩子的人身安全并远离帮派麻烦一样，我们这些中上层白领家庭的父母需要格外努力，确保我们的孩子不会被

